export const PRIVACY_HTML: string = "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>ShogunAgent Privacy Policy</title>\n<style>body{font:16px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:46rem;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;color:#111;background:#fff}\n@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{color:#e8e8e8;background:#111}a{color:#8ab4ff}th,td{border-color:#444}}\ntable{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.92rem}th,td{border:1px solid #ccc;padding:.4rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}\nblockquote{margin:1rem 0;padding:.5rem 1rem;border-left:4px solid #c90;background:rgba(200,150,0,.08)}code{font-size:.9em;word-break:break-word}</style>\n</head><body>\n<h1 id=\"shogunagent-privacy-policy\">ShogunAgent Privacy Policy</h1>\n<blockquote><p><strong>DRAFT — for legal review. Not in force.</strong> This draft describes what the code in this repository actually does as of 2026-10-25. Counsel must review it, fill the placeholders marked <code>[TBD]</code>, and confirm the subprocessor list before it is published. Until then, <code>/privacy</code> serves this text with the same banner.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Last updated: 2026-10-25 (draft). Operator: Select KK <code>[TBD: registered address, company number]</code>. Contact: <code>[TBD: privacy@ address]</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-shogunagent-is\">1. What ShogunAgent is</h2>\n<p>ShogunAgent is a state layer for AI agents: a typed model of a person&#39;s work (people, projects, commitments, open loops) that agents read and write over MCP, REST and a CLI. It runs in two ways:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Local (open source).</strong> The engine runs on your own machine or server. Nothing in this policy applies to data that never leaves it: we do not receive it.</li><li><strong>Hosted.</strong> We run the engine and a gateway for you at <code>api.shogunagent.dev</code>. This policy covers the hosted service.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-short-version\">2. The short version</h2>\n<ul><li>Your state lives in <strong>one encrypted store per principal</strong> (one SQLCipher file per person). Other customers cannot query it; access across principals happens only through access lists you set up.</li><li>The <strong>gateway never stores request or response bodies.</strong> Logs, metrics, billing records and error reports carry ids, counts, amounts and timestamps, never the content of what your agents send or receive.</li><li><strong>We do not train models on your data,</strong> and we do not let our model providers do so.</li><li><strong>Export is always free</strong> and available at any time, in an open, documented format. Deleting your data is free too.</li><li><strong>No telemetry by default.</strong> Anything beyond what is needed to run and bill the service is opt-in and counts only.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-we-process-and-why\">3. What we process, and why</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Data</th><th>Where it lives</th><th>Why</th><th>How long</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Your state and memory (events you ingest, notes, the people / projects / commitments / open loops derived from them, their provenance)</td><td>Your principal&#39;s encrypted store</td><td>To provide the service you asked for</td><td>Until you delete it or close the principal</td></tr><tr><td>Request and response bodies passing through the gateway (<code>/mcp</code>, router runs)</td><td>Relayed in memory only; never written to disk, logs or metrics</td><td>To forward the call</td><td>Not retained</td></tr><tr><td>Account identifiers: principal id, tenant id, an optional referrer id you supply</td><td>Gateway database</td><td>To identify who owns a store and a wallet</td><td>While the principal exists</td></tr><tr><td>API keys</td><td>Gateway database, as a salted PBKDF2-SHA256 hash plus a short lookup prefix; the key itself is shown to you once and never stored</td><td>Authentication</td><td>Until the key is revoked or expires, then as long as the principal exists</td></tr><tr><td>OAuth grants for apps you connect (the app&#39;s client id URL, its redirect URI, granted scopes; codes and tokens only as SHA-256 hashes)</td><td>Gateway database</td><td>Letting MCP clients connect on your behalf</td><td>Codes: 60 seconds plus one day; tokens: until expiry plus one day</td></tr><tr><td>Billing records: wallet balance, top-ups, per-call receipts (method, tool name, unit, quantity, cost, time), disputes, monthly statements, commit contracts</td><td>Gateway database</td><td>Metering, invoicing, disputes, tax and accounting</td><td>Receipts: 400 days; closed statements and ledger entries: <code>[TBD: statutory accounting retention]</code></td></tr><tr><td>Dispute reasons you write</td><td>Gateway database (bounded to 1,000 characters)</td><td>Resolving the dispute</td><td>With the dispute record; never copied into statements</td></tr><tr><td>Payment details</td><td>Held by Stripe, not by us; we keep Stripe&#39;s session, payment and charge ids</td><td>Taking card top-ups and refunds</td><td>As billing records</td></tr><tr><td>Client IP address at registration</td><td>Used for per-IP rate limiting, stored only as a SHA-256 hash</td><td>Abuse prevention</td><td>About ten minutes after the one-hour window closes</td></tr><tr><td>Request log lines (method, route template, status, duration, a fixed event word, a hash of the key id)</td><td>Our hosting provider&#39;s log store</td><td>Operating and securing the service</td><td><code>[TBD: log retention of the hosting provider]</code></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>We do not sell personal data, we do not use it for advertising, and we do not build profiles of you beyond what you put into your own state.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-model-providers-and-zero-retention\">4. Model providers and zero retention</h2>\n<p>Some operations (ingest extraction, consolidation, AI search) call a large-language-model provider. We send only the chunk needed for that call, under zero-retention terms where the provider offers them, and we never allow the provider to train on it. Bodies are relayed, not stored, on our side. Which provider handles which operation is listed in section 7.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-your-choices-and-rights\">5. Your choices and rights</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Export:</strong> <code>GET /v1/export</code> (or the CLI / MCP equivalent) returns your full state in the documented export format (<code>docs/api/export-v1.md</code>). It is free and never rate-limited into uselessness.</li><li><strong>Delete:</strong> deleting a principal deletes its store. Billing records we must keep by law are kept without your state content.</li><li><strong>Revoke access:</strong> revoke any API key or OAuth grant at any time; tokens minted from a revoked key stop working immediately.</li><li><strong>Statutory rights:</strong> depending on where you live (for example the EU/UK GDPR, the California CCPA/CPRA, Japan&#39;s APPI) you can ask to access, correct, delete or port your personal data, or object to processing. Write to <code>[TBD: privacy@ address]</code>. <code>[TBD: EU/UK representative, if required]</code>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"6-security\">6. Security</h2>\n<p>Per-principal encrypted stores, keys stored only as hashes, secrets held in a dedicated type and never logged, tenant keys from a key management service or the environment, and a gateway that is structurally unable to log bodies. Report a vulnerability to <code>[TBD: security@ address]</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-subprocessors\">7. Subprocessors</h2>\n<p><code>[TBD: confirm each entry, its region and the contract in place before publishing.]</code></p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Subprocessor</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Data</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cloudflare, Inc. <code>[TBD]</code></td><td>Gateway hosting (Workers), TLS, request logs</td><td>Metadata in transit; log lines as described above</td></tr><tr><td><code>[TBD: managed Postgres provider]</code></td><td>Gateway database</td><td>Account, key-hash, billing and OAuth records</td></tr><tr><td><code>[TBD: host of the per-principal stores]</code></td><td>Hosted engine and encrypted stores</td><td>Your state</td></tr><tr><td>Stripe, Inc.</td><td>Card payments, refunds, metered commit billing</td><td>Payment details (held by Stripe), amounts, ids</td></tr><tr><td><code>[TBD: model providers, e.g. OpenAI, Anthropic]</code></td><td>Extraction, consolidation, AI search</td><td>The chunk needed for one call, under zero retention</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>We will give <code>[TBD: notice period]</code> notice before adding a subprocessor that processes your state.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-international-transfers\">8. International transfers</h2>\n<p><code>[TBD: transfer mechanism (SCCs / UK addendum / APPI cross-border rules) and the regions used.]</code></p>\n<h2 id=\"9-children\">9. Children</h2>\n<p>The service is not directed to children under 16 and we do not knowingly collect their data.</p>\n<h2 id=\"10-changes\">10. Changes</h2>\n<p>We will post changes here and, for material changes, notify account holders <code>[TBD: how and how far in advance]</code>.</p>\n</body></html>\n";
export const TERMS_HTML: string = "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>ShogunAgent Terms of Service</title>\n<style>body{font:16px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:46rem;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;color:#111;background:#fff}\n@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{color:#e8e8e8;background:#111}a{color:#8ab4ff}th,td{border-color:#444}}\ntable{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.92rem}th,td{border:1px solid #ccc;padding:.4rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}\nblockquote{margin:1rem 0;padding:.5rem 1rem;border-left:4px solid #c90;background:rgba(200,150,0,.08)}code{font-size:.9em;word-break:break-word}</style>\n</head><body>\n<h1 id=\"shogunagent-terms-of-service\">ShogunAgent Terms of Service</h1>\n<blockquote><p><strong>DRAFT — for legal review. Not in force.</strong> This draft is written to match how the service is actually built and billed as of 2026-10-25. Counsel must review it, fill the placeholders marked <code>[TBD]</code>, and settle governing law before it is published. Until then, <code>/terms</code> serves this text with the same banner.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Last updated: 2026-10-25 (draft). These terms are between you and Select KK <code>[TBD: registered address]</code> (&quot;we&quot;).</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-the-service\">1. The service</h2>\n<p>ShogunAgent gives AI agents a shared, typed model of a person&#39;s work, read and written over MCP, REST and a CLI, with permission tiers on every action. The open-source engine is licensed separately under Apache-2.0; these terms cover the <strong>hosted</strong> service at <code>api.shogunagent.dev</code> and its gateway, router and wallet.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-accounts-principals-and-keys\">2. Accounts, principals and keys</h2>\n<ul><li>An agent may register a principal itself (<code>POST /v1/agents</code>). Whoever controls that principal&#39;s keys is responsible for its use. If an agent registers on your behalf, you are bound by these terms when you use the result.</li><li>Keep keys secret. A key is shown once; we store only its hash and cannot recover it. Revoke a key you believe is exposed; access tokens minted from it stop working at once.</li><li>Apps you connect over OAuth act with the scopes you granted and never with <code>admin</code>. You can revoke a grant at any time.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-permission-tiers\">3. Permission tiers</h2>\n<p>Reads are L1. Writes to your own data are L2. Anything that sends, posts, pays or creates something outside your store is L3 and waits for an explicit human approval. We do not offer a setting that turns an L3 action into an automatic one. You are responsible for the approvals you give.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-prices-wallet-and-payment\">4. Prices, wallet and payment</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Usage-based, no subscription.</strong> Prices are published at <code>/.well-known/pricing.json</code>, which is generated from the same table that meters your calls. State reads, remote approvals, export and deletion cost nothing.</li><li><strong>Prepaid wallet.</strong> Metered calls draw from a prepaid micro-USD balance. When the balance or a hard cap would be exceeded, the call is refused with <code>402</code> before anything happens; nothing is charged for a refused call, and failed calls and empty results cost nothing.</li><li><strong>Caps.</strong> Every key and principal has daily and monthly hard caps. Defaults are conservative; you may lower them at any time.</li><li><strong>Top-ups</strong> are taken by card through Stripe. A new principal receives a free allowance, which has no cash value.</li><li><strong>Annual commits.</strong> An enterprise customer may agree a commit contract (an amount over a period) with a 10–25% discount, applied to every metered call during the period; receipts show both prices. Usage past the committed amount is billed monthly in arrears at the same discount, if the contract says so. <code>[TBD: invoicing, payment terms and what happens to unused commit at the end of the period.]</code></li><li><strong>Statements and disputes.</strong> You can download a monthly statement in JSON or CSV at any time; once a month closes, its statement is fixed and carries a checksum. Dispute any receipt through the API within <code>[TBD: dispute window, e.g. 60 days]</code>; a dispute we accept is credited to your wallet.</li><li><strong>Refunds.</strong> Unused prepaid balance is refundable <code>[TBD: conditions]</code>; refunds go back to the original card through Stripe and are deducted from the wallet.</li><li><strong>Taxes.</strong> Prices exclude taxes. <code>[TBD: tax handling.]</code></li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"5-your-data\">5. Your data</h2>\n<p>You own your data. We process it only to provide the service, as described in the Privacy Policy (<code>/privacy</code>). We do not train models on it. You can export everything, free, at any time, in the documented open format, and delete it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-acceptable-use\">6. Acceptable use</h2>\n<p>Do not use the service to break the law, to infringe others&#39; rights, to process data you have no right to process, to attack or overload the service or the providers behind the router, or to evade caps, rate limits or permission tiers. We may suspend a key or principal that does, with notice where we can give it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-third-party-tools-through-the-router\">7. Third-party tools through the router</h2>\n<p>The router can run tools operated by third parties. Their price, behaviour and terms are theirs; the router publishes each tool&#39;s price and ranking breakdown and labels our own first-party tools. A tool run that fails costs nothing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-availability-and-changes\">8. Availability and changes</h2>\n<p>We publish live status at <code>/.well-known/status.json</code>. We aim for high availability but, outside a signed enterprise agreement, offer no service-level guarantee. We may change prices with <code>[TBD: notice period]</code> notice published in <code>/.well-known/pricing.json</code>; a call is always charged at the price in force when it was made.</p>\n<h2 id=\"9-warranties-and-liability\">9. Warranties and liability</h2>\n<p><code>[TBD: disclaimer of warranties, limitation of liability (for example capped at the amounts paid in the preceding twelve months), indemnities.]</code></p>\n<h2 id=\"10-termination\">10. Termination</h2>\n<p>You may stop using the service at any time; export first. We may terminate for material breach. On termination, unused prepaid balance is handled as in section 4, and your data is deleted after <code>[TBD: grace period]</code> unless the law requires us to keep billing records.</p>\n<h2 id=\"11-governing-law\">11. Governing law</h2>\n<p><code>[TBD: governing law and venue.]</code></p>\n<h2 id=\"12-contact\">12. Contact</h2>\n<p><code>[TBD: support and legal contact addresses.]</code></p>\n</body></html>\n";
export const DPA_HTML: string = "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>ShogunAgent Data Processing Addendum</title>\n<style>body{font:16px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:46rem;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;color:#111;background:#fff}\n@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{color:#e8e8e8;background:#111}a{color:#8ab4ff}th,td{border-color:#444}}\ntable{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.92rem}th,td{border:1px solid #ccc;padding:.4rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}\nblockquote{margin:1rem 0;padding:.5rem 1rem;border-left:4px solid #c90;background:rgba(200,150,0,.08)}code{font-size:.9em;word-break:break-word}</style>\n</head><body>\n<h1 id=\"shogunagent-data-processing-addendum\">ShogunAgent Data Processing Addendum</h1>\n<blockquote><p><strong>DRAFT — for legal review. Not in force.</strong> This draft describes what the code in this repository actually does as of 2026-10-25. Counsel must review it, fill the placeholders marked <code>[TBD]</code>, attach the Standard Contractual Clauses and confirm the subprocessor list before it is offered to any customer. Until then, <code>/legal/dpa</code> serves this text with the same banner.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Last updated: 2026-10-25 (draft). This Data Processing Addendum (&quot;DPA&quot;) forms part of the agreement between the customer named in the order form (&quot;Customer&quot;) and Select KK <code>[TBD: registered address, company number]</code> (&quot;we&quot;) for the hosted ShogunAgent service (the &quot;Agreement&quot;). Where this DPA and the Agreement disagree about personal data, this DPA wins.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-roles\">1. Roles</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Customer is the controller</strong> of the personal data its principals, agents and people put into the service (&quot;Customer Data&quot;). Customer decides what is ingested, which agents may read and write it, and how long it is kept.</li><li><strong>We are the processor.</strong> We process Customer Data only to provide the service, on Customer&#39;s documented instructions. The Agreement, this DPA and Customer&#39;s use of the API (including its permission tiers, access lists and deletion calls) are those instructions.</li><li><strong>Where we act as a controller.</strong> For account, billing and security records (principal and tenant ids, key hashes, receipts, invoices, request log lines), we are a controller, and the Privacy Policy (<code>/privacy</code>) applies.</li><li>If an instruction would, in our opinion, breach data protection law, we will tell Customer and need not follow it.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"2-what-is-processed\">2. What is processed</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Subject matter</strong></td><td>Hosting a typed, provenance-backed model of people&#39;s work (people, projects, commitments, open loops) that AI agents read and write.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Duration</strong></td><td>The term of the Agreement, plus the deletion periods in section 8.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Nature and purpose</strong></td><td>Storing, deriving, searching and returning Customer Data; enforcing permission tiers; relaying calls to tools and model providers chosen by Customer; metering and billing.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Categories of data subjects</strong></td><td>Customer&#39;s principals (usually employees or contractors), and people who appear in the data Customer ingests (colleagues, customers, correspondents).</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Categories of personal data</strong></td><td>Whatever Customer ingests (names, e-mail addresses, message and document excerpts, meeting notes, tasks), the state derived from it, and identity metadata for provisioning: SCIM user attributes (userName, name, e-mail addresses, active flag, external id) and tenant admin e-mail addresses.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Special categories</strong></td><td>Not intended. Customer must not ingest special-category data unless the order form says otherwise <code>[TBD]</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"3-how-we-protect-it\">3. How we protect it</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>One encrypted store per principal.</strong> Each principal&#39;s state is a separate SQLCipher file with its own key (hosted keys sealed by a key management service; customer-managed keys where the order form provides BYOC or KMS). Cross-principal reads happen only through access lists Customer sets up (Teams), never by query.</li><li><strong>Bodies are not retained outside the store.</strong> The gateway relays request and response bodies without writing them to disk, logs, metrics or error reports. The exact paths and the metadata we do keep are listed in the Zero-Retention Commitment (<code>/legal/zdr</code>).</li><li><strong>Secrets are stored hashed or sealed.</strong> API keys as salted PBKDF2-SHA256 hashes, OAuth codes and tokens and the SCIM bearer as SHA-256 hashes, OIDC client secrets sealed with AES-256-GCM.</li><li><strong>Access control.</strong> Every action carries a permission tier (L1 read, L2 write to Customer&#39;s own data, L3 external send, payment or calendar creation, which always waits for a human). Tenant admins sign in through Customer&#39;s own identity provider (OIDC or SAML 2.0); SCIM deactivation revokes a principal&#39;s keys and app grants at once.</li><li><strong>Personnel.</strong> People with access to production are bound by confidentiality and get access on a need-to-know basis <code>[TBD: access review cadence]</code>.</li><li>A fuller description of technical and organisational measures is in Annex II <code>[TBD: attach]</code>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-subprocessors\">4. Subprocessors</h2>\n<p>Customer authorises the subprocessors below. We will give at least <code>[TBD: 30]</code> days&#39; notice of a new subprocessor that processes Customer Data, by <code>[TBD: e-mail to the tenant admins / a subscribable page]</code>; Customer may object on reasonable data protection grounds, and if we cannot resolve the objection Customer may terminate the affected service and receive a pro-rata refund of prepaid amounts.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Subprocessor</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Customer Data processed</th><th>Region</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cloudflare, Inc. <code>[TBD: confirm]</code></td><td>Gateway hosting (Workers), TLS termination</td><td>Bodies in transit only; never stored</td><td>The tenant&#39;s region <code>[TBD]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>[TBD: managed Postgres provider]</code></td><td>Gateway database</td><td>Identity and billing metadata; SCIM attributes; no state content</td><td>The tenant&#39;s region</td></tr><tr><td><code>[TBD: host of the per-principal stores]</code></td><td>Encrypted per-principal stores and the engine</td><td>All Customer Data</td><td>The tenant&#39;s region</td></tr><tr><td>Stripe, Inc.</td><td>Card payments, refunds, metered commit billing</td><td>None (billing contacts only, under the Privacy Policy)</td><td><code>[TBD]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>[TBD: model providers, e.g. OpenAI, Anthropic]</code></td><td>Extraction, consolidation, AI search</td><td>The chunk needed for one call, under zero retention (<code>/legal/zdr</code>)</td><td><code>[TBD]</code></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Tools that Customer&#39;s agents choose to run through the router are not our subprocessors: Customer selects them, and the router shows each tool&#39;s operator and terms.</p>\n<p>We impose data protection terms on each subprocessor that are no less protective than this DPA, and remain liable for their performance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-regions-and-international-transfers\">5. Regions and international transfers</h2>\n<ul><li>A tenant is created in one region (<code>us</code>, <code>eu</code>, <code>jp</code>, …) and its principals are served only by that region&#39;s deployment. Each region has its own gateway, database, engine hosts and keys; deployments share no database and never call each other, so Customer Data does not move between regions in the normal course of the service. A request that reaches the wrong region is refused and told which endpoint to use; it is not forwarded.</li><li>Where Customer Data is nevertheless transferred out of the EEA, the UK or Switzerland (for example to a model provider or for support), the parties agree to the Standard Contractual Clauses (Commission Implementing Decision (EU) 2021/914), Module Two (controller to processor) and, where applicable, Module Three, with the UK International Data Transfer Addendum and the Swiss amendments <code>[TBD: attach as Annex III, with docking clause, governing law and supervisory authority selections]</code>. For transfers out of Japan, <code>[TBD: APPI Article 28 basis]</code>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"6-assistance-and-audits\">6. Assistance and audits</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Data subject requests.</strong> The API gives Customer the tools to answer most requests itself: export (<code>GET /v1/export</code>, free), correction through the write tools, deletion of a principal, and SCIM for joiners and leavers. We forward to Customer any request we receive directly, and help with the rest at no charge <code>[TBD: reasonable-effort wording]</code>.</li><li><strong>Impact assessments and consultations.</strong> We provide the information we reasonably hold.</li><li><strong>Audits.</strong> We make available our <code>[TBD: SOC 2 Type II / ISO 27001 reports when available]</code> and this repository&#39;s open-source engine. Customer may audit once a year on <code>[TBD: notice]</code> notice, at its cost, under confidentiality, or more often after a personal data breach. Tenant admins can export the audit log of every member principal at any time (<code>GET /v1/tenant/audit/export</code>).</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"7-personal-data-breaches\">7. Personal data breaches</h2>\n<p>We will notify Customer <strong>without undue delay and in any case within <code>[TBD: 48]</code> hours</strong> of becoming aware of a personal data breach affecting Customer Data, to the tenant admins and the security contact in the order form. The notice will describe, as far as known, the nature of the breach, the categories and approximate number of data subjects and records, the likely consequences, and the measures taken or proposed. We will update it as we learn more, take reasonable steps to contain the breach, and not tell third parties about it on Customer&#39;s behalf without Customer&#39;s consent unless the law requires us to.</p>\n<h2 id=\"8-deletion-and-export\">8. Deletion and export</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Export, any time, free.</strong> Customer can export every principal&#39;s full state in the documented open format (<code>docs/api/export-v1.md</code>) during the term and for <code>[TBD: 30]</code> days after it ends.</li><li><strong>Deleting a person.</strong> A SCIM <code>DELETE</code> of a user revokes the principal&#39;s keys and app grants immediately and schedules deletion of its store after a grace period (30 days by default, configurable per deployment from 1 to 365 days) during which Customer can still export it. A tombstone (ids and times, no attributes) is kept so the deletion is not undone by a retry.</li><li><strong>End of the Agreement.</strong> We delete Customer Data within <code>[TBD: 30]</code> days after the export window closes, including backups within <code>[TBD: backup rotation period]</code>, unless the law requires us to keep it, in which case we keep it isolated and only for as long as required.</li><li><strong>What is kept.</strong> Billing records (receipts, statements, ledger entries) carry ids, amounts and times, never state content, and are kept as the law requires.</li><li>On request we will certify the deletion in writing.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"9-liability-and-precedence\">9. Liability and precedence</h2>\n<p>Each party&#39;s liability under this DPA is subject to the limitations in the Agreement <code>[TBD: confirm carve-outs]</code>. If the Standard Contractual Clauses apply, they prevail over this DPA and the Agreement where they conflict.</p>\n<h2 id=\"annexes\">Annexes</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Annex I</strong> — list of parties, description of the processing (section 2 above), competent supervisory authority <code>[TBD]</code>.</li><li><strong>Annex II</strong> — technical and organisational measures <code>[TBD]</code>.</li><li><strong>Annex III</strong> — Standard Contractual Clauses and transfer addenda <code>[TBD]</code>.</li></ul>\n</body></html>\n";
export const ZDR_HTML: string = "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>ShogunAgent Zero-Retention Commitment</title>\n<style>body{font:16px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:46rem;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;color:#111;background:#fff}\n@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{color:#e8e8e8;background:#111}a{color:#8ab4ff}th,td{border-color:#444}}\ntable{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.92rem}th,td{border:1px solid #ccc;padding:.4rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}\nblockquote{margin:1rem 0;padding:.5rem 1rem;border-left:4px solid #c90;background:rgba(200,150,0,.08)}code{font-size:.9em;word-break:break-word}</style>\n</head><body>\n<h1 id=\"shogunagent-zero-retention-commitment\">ShogunAgent Zero-Retention Commitment</h1>\n<blockquote><p><strong>DRAFT — for legal review. Not in force.</strong> This draft describes what the code in this repository actually does as of 2026-10-25. Counsel must review it and the provider rows marked <code>[TBD]</code> must be confirmed in writing with each provider before it is offered to any customer. Until then, <code>/legal/zdr</code> serves this text with the same banner.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Last updated: 2026-10-25 (draft). This commitment is part of the Data Processing Addendum (<code>/legal/dpa</code>) for hosted ShogunAgent. It says exactly where your content is <strong>not</strong> kept, what metadata <strong>is</strong> kept, and for how long.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-the-commitment\">1. The commitment</h2>\n<p>Your content — what your agents ingest, the state derived from it, and the bodies of the requests and responses that pass through the hosted service — is stored <strong>only in your principal&#39;s encrypted store</strong>. No other part of the hosted service writes it to disk, a database, a log, a metric, a trace, an error report or a cache. Streams are relayed as they arrive, not buffered whole.</p>\n<p>This is enforced in code, not only in policy: the gateway&#39;s only console writer (<code>apps/api/src/logging.ts</code>) accepts nothing but fixed words, route templates, status codes, durations, counters and hashed ids, and has tests that fail if a body, token or identifier reaches a log line.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-paths-that-retain-nothing\">2. Paths that retain nothing</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Path</th><th>What passes through</th><th>What is kept</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>/mcp</code> (the metered MCP proxy)</td><td>JSON-RPC request and response bodies, and streams</td><td>Nothing of the body. The call is classified from headers, never from the body. A receipt (method, tool name, unit, quantity, cost, time) is kept for billing.</td></tr><tr><td>Router runs (<code>/v1/router/run</code>)</td><td>Tool inputs and outputs</td><td>Nothing of the body. A receipt as above; provider credentials are injected at the transport layer and never reach the router engine.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /v1/handoff</code></td><td>The state-of-work object built from one recall</td><td>Nothing; it is built per request and returned.</td></tr><tr><td>Remote approvals (<code>/approve/{token}</code>, Slack, webhooks)</td><td>A redacted summary fetched from your store</td><td>Nothing; the summary is rendered or delivered and dropped. Webhook endpoint URLs and event names you register are kept until you delete them.</td></tr><tr><td>Tenant audit export (<code>GET /v1/tenant/audit/export</code>)</td><td>Each member&#39;s audit records</td><td>Nothing; bytes are streamed from the store to the admin and never parsed beyond the per-member frame.</td></tr><tr><td>SSO sign-in (OIDC ID tokens, SAML assertions)</td><td>Identity claims from your IdP</td><td>Nothing of the token or assertion. The admin&#39;s IdP subject is bound once; a SAML assertion id is kept only as a SHA-256 hash until the assertion expires (minutes), to refuse replays.</td></tr><tr><td>SCIM provisioning</td><td>User and group resources from your IdP</td><td>Only the attributes the protocol needs: userName, name, e-mail addresses, external id, active flag, group names and members. Nothing else in the request (for example passwords or enterprise-extension attributes) is kept. Deleted users keep a tombstone of ids and times.</td></tr><tr><td>Model calls (extraction, consolidation, AI search)</td><td>The chunk needed for one call</td><td>Nothing on our side; provider-side retention is in section 4.</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"3-metadata-we-keep-and-for-how-long\">3. Metadata we keep, and for how long</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Metadata</th><th>Contents</th><th>Kept for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Request log lines</td><td>Method, route template (ids collapsed), status, duration, a fixed event word, a 12-character hash of the key id</td><td><code>[TBD: the hosting provider&#39;s log retention, set short in its dashboard]</code></td></tr><tr><td>Status window</td><td>Counts and latency percentiles of recent requests, in memory</td><td>A rolling window inside one running instance; never written out</td></tr><tr><td>Receipts</td><td>Method, tool name, unit, quantity, cost, time, ids</td><td>400 days (a year of statements plus a dispute window); unsettled receipts until settled</td></tr><tr><td>Statements and ledger entries</td><td>Totals, amounts, ids, a checksum</td><td><code>[TBD: statutory accounting retention]</code></td></tr><tr><td>API keys</td><td>Salted PBKDF2-SHA256 hash and a 10-character lookup prefix</td><td>Until the principal is deleted</td></tr><tr><td>OAuth codes and tokens</td><td>SHA-256 hashes, client id URL, scopes, times</td><td>Codes: 60 seconds plus one day; tokens: until expiry plus one day</td></tr><tr><td>Registration proof-of-work</td><td>SHA-256 of the spent challenge</td><td>Five minutes (the challenge&#39;s lifetime)</td></tr><tr><td>Per-IP rate limiting</td><td>SHA-256 of the client IP</td><td>About ten minutes after the one-hour window closes</td></tr><tr><td>SAML replay cache</td><td>SHA-256 of tenant and assertion id</td><td>Until the assertion expires</td></tr><tr><td>Tenant directory</td><td>Tenant name, region, plan, SSO configuration (OIDC client secret sealed with AES-256-GCM), SCIM bearer as SHA-256, admin e-mail addresses, SCIM attributes</td><td>While the tenant exists; SCIM attributes are cleared when a user is deleted</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>None of this metadata contains the content of your state or of a request or response body.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-model-providers\">4. Model providers</h2>\n<p>We send a provider only the chunk needed for one call, never train on it and never let the provider train on it. Provider-side zero retention must be in place for each provider before it carries customer traffic:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Operations</th><th>Zero-retention status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>[TBD: OpenAI]</code></td><td><code>[TBD]</code></td><td><code>[TBD: ZDR approval reference; store:false on every call]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>[TBD: Anthropic]</code></td><td><code>[TBD]</code></td><td><code>[TBD: zero-retention agreement reference]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>[TBD: Azure OpenAI]</code></td><td><code>[TBD]</code></td><td><code>[TBD: modified abuse monitoring exemption; no customer traffic until approved]</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>[TBD: others]</code></td><td><code>[TBD]</code></td><td><code>[TBD]</code></td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Tools run through the router are operated by third parties Customer chooses; their retention is theirs and is shown on each tool&#39;s listing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-exceptions\">5. Exceptions</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Your store.</strong> By design it keeps what you ingest, until you delete it. Deleting a principal deletes its store (after the SCIM grace period when deletion comes from your IdP).</li><li><strong>Legal holds.</strong> If the law compels us to preserve specific content, we will tell Customer unless the law forbids it.</li><li><strong>Abuse.</strong> We do not inspect bodies to detect abuse; rate limits, caps and per-key revocation work on metadata alone.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"6-verification\">6. Verification</h2>\n<p>Customer may review the open-source gateway and engine in this repository, the log hygiene tests, and on request <code>[TBD: third-party audit reports]</code>. A change that would retain content on any path in section 2 is a material change to this commitment and needs <code>[TBD: notice period]</code> notice.</p>\n</body></html>\n";
export const SLA_HTML: string = "<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title>ShogunAgent Service Level Agreement</title>\n<style>body{font:16px/1.6 system-ui,sans-serif;max-width:46rem;margin:2rem auto;padding:0 1rem;color:#111;background:#fff}\n@media (prefers-color-scheme:dark){body{color:#e8e8e8;background:#111}a{color:#8ab4ff}th,td{border-color:#444}}\ntable{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:.92rem}th,td{border:1px solid #ccc;padding:.4rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top}\nblockquote{margin:1rem 0;padding:.5rem 1rem;border-left:4px solid #c90;background:rgba(200,150,0,.08)}code{font-size:.9em;word-break:break-word}</style>\n</head><body>\n<h1 id=\"shogunagent-service-level-agreement\">ShogunAgent Service Level Agreement</h1>\n<blockquote><p><strong>DRAFT — for legal review. Not in force.</strong> This draft is written to match how the hosted service is built and measured as of 2026-10-25. Counsel must review it, fill the placeholders marked <code>[TBD]</code>, and confirm the targets with operations before it is offered to any customer. Until then, <code>/legal/sla</code> serves this text with the same banner.</p></blockquote>\n<p>Last updated: 2026-10-25 (draft). This SLA applies to customers with a signed enterprise agreement (the &quot;Agreement&quot;) that refers to it. Self-serve use has no service-level guarantee (Terms, section 8).</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-availability-target\">1. Availability target</h2>\n<p>We aim for <strong>99.9% monthly availability</strong> of the hosted service in the customer&#39;s region <code>[TBD: 99.95% for an annual commit above a threshold]</code>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Covered endpoints:</strong> the metered MCP endpoint (<code>/mcp</code>), the REST state and memory routes, the router verbs, key and wallet routes, SCIM and SSO.</li><li><strong>Available</strong> means the endpoint answers a well-formed, authenticated request with something other than a 5xx status, within 30 seconds, as measured by our external probes in the customer&#39;s region every minute.</li><li><strong>Monthly availability</strong> = (minutes in the month − minutes unavailable) ÷ minutes in the month. A minute is unavailable when more than 5% of probe and customer requests in it fail as above.</li><li>Live status and incident history are published at <code>/.well-known/status.json</code> and <code>/health</code>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"2-service-credits\">2. Service credits</h2>\n<p>If monthly availability falls below the target, Customer receives a credit against the next invoice or wallet, as a share of that month&#39;s fees for the affected region:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Monthly availability</th><th>Credit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Below 99.9%, at least 99.0%</td><td>10%</td></tr><tr><td>Below 99.0%, at least 95.0%</td><td>25%</td></tr><tr><td>Below 95.0%</td><td>50%</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<ul><li>Customer requests the credit within 30 days of the end of the month, through support, with the affected region and times.</li><li>Credits are capped at 50% of the month&#39;s fees for the affected region, cannot be exchanged for cash, and are Customer&#39;s sole remedy for unavailability <code>[TBD: confirm against the Agreement&#39;s liability terms]</code>.</li><li>Fees here mean usage charges and the pro-rated share of an annual commit; third-party tool prices passed through the router are excluded.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-exclusions\">3. Exclusions</h2>\n<p>Unavailability does not count when it is caused by:</p>\n<ul><li>scheduled maintenance announced at least <code>[TBD: 72]</code> hours ahead, up to <code>[TBD: 4]</code> hours a month, outside <code>[TBD: business hours in the customer&#39;s region]</code>;</li><li>Customer&#39;s own systems, network, identity provider or SCIM client, or a configuration Customer controls (caps, revoked keys, an SSO setup that refuses sign-in);</li><li>refusals the service is designed to give: <code>402</code> for an empty wallet or a reached cap, <code>401</code> / <code>403</code> for invalid credentials or scopes, <code>421 wrong_region</code> for a request sent to another region, <code>429</code> rate limits, and approvals waiting for a human;</li><li>third-party tools run through the router, and model providers&#39; outages beyond our control where no alternative provider is configured;</li><li>suspension under the Agreement for breach, non-payment or abuse;</li><li>force majeure, or a beta or preview feature marked as such.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"4-support-tiers\">4. Support tiers</h2>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Standard (self-serve)</th><th>Enterprise</th><th>Enterprise Premium <code>[TBD]</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Channel</td><td>E-mail, issue tracker</td><td>E-mail, shared channel</td><td>E-mail, shared channel, phone for Severity 1</td></tr><tr><td>Hours</td><td>Business days <code>[TBD: time zone]</code></td><td>24×7 for Severity 1, business days otherwise</td><td>24×7</td></tr><tr><td>First response, Severity 1 (service down or data at risk)</td><td>Best effort</td><td>1 hour</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>First response, Severity 2 (major feature impaired)</td><td>2 business days</td><td>4 business hours</td><td>2 hours</td></tr><tr><td>First response, Severity 3 (minor issue, question)</td><td>Best effort</td><td>1 business day</td><td>4 business hours</td></tr><tr><td>Named contact, quarterly review</td><td>—</td><td>—</td><td>Yes</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p>Customer assigns severity when opening a ticket; we may adjust it with an explanation. Response times measure the first human response, not resolution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"5-incidents\">5. Incidents</h2>\n<p>For every Severity 1 incident we publish a status entry while it lasts and a post-incident report within <code>[TBD: 5]</code> business days, covering impact, timeline, cause and follow-up actions. Security incidents affecting personal data follow the breach-notice terms of the Data Processing Addendum (<code>/legal/dpa</code>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"6-changes\">6. Changes</h2>\n<p>We may update this SLA with <code>[TBD: 90]</code> days&#39; notice; a change that lowers the target or the credits does not apply before the current term of the Agreement renews.</p>\n</body></html>\n";
